<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/expert/data-engineering-and-pipelines">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data engineering builds <strong>the reliable plumbing that turns raw events into trustworthy
    tables</strong> — the layer every analyst, dashboard and model stands on. Its promise: data
    that is fresh, correct, documented and reproducible. Its reality: most "data science project"
    failures are data engineering failures in disguise, because a model is only as good as the
    table it trained on, and a dashboard is only as truthful as the last successful run of the
    job that built it. The discipline exists because production data systems differ from notebook
    analytics in one decisive way: the data <em>keeps arriving</em>, the schemas <em>keep
    drifting</em>, and the consumers <em>keep assuming</em> yesterday's assumptions. Engineering
    is what makes the pipeline survive all three.
  </p>
  <p>
    The economics justify the discipline. Industry surveys (Anaconda's State of Data Science
    series) consistently put around 40–45% of a data scientist's working time on data preparation
    — loading and cleaning — before any modelling happens. An organisation that industrialises
    that preparation once, in tested pipelines, buys back nearly half its analytical capacity;
    one that leaves it to individuals repeats the same cleaning work in every notebook, with
    every analyst's version of "clean" quietly different.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four pillars of production data plumbing" widget="equationStep" config={pipeCfg} />

  <h2>What data science is — and where the plumbing sits</h2>
  <p>
    The Data 8 framing this course follows describes data science as a cycle: <strong>form a
    question, acquire and parse data, explore and clean it, build models and draw inferences,
    then communicate the result</strong> — with an obligation running underneath the whole cycle
    to consider the impact of the work on the people the data describes. Data engineering is the
    discipline that makes the middle of that cycle <em>repeatable</em>. Everything before it is
    one-off craft; everything after it is modelling and communication. The engineering layer's
    deliverable is a set of tables whose semantics (what a row means), freshness (how far behind
    reality they run), and correctness (what has been tested) are explicit — so that a data
    scientist asking "is this number right?" gets an answer from a document and a test suite
    instead of an afternoon of archaeology. The work is also where scale becomes real: an
    analysis of 10 million rows is a notebook; a system that ingests 10 million rows
    <em>per hour</em>, forever, with late arrivals and duplicate deliveries, is an engineering
    problem with solutions this chapter unpacks.
  </p>

  <h2>From ETL to ELT: the load order that changed the field</h2>
  <p>
    The classic pipeline was <strong>ETL</strong>: Extract from sources, Transform into clean
    shape, then Load into the warehouse — transform first, because warehouses were expensive and
    only clean, narrow tables were worth storing. Cloud warehouses (Snowflake, BigQuery,
    Redshift) inverted the economics: storage is cheap and compute scales elastically, so the
    modern pattern is <strong>ELT</strong> — load the raw data first, in its original messy form,
    and transform it <em>inside</em> the warehouse with SQL. The payoff is more than fashion:
    keeping raw data permanently means every transform is re-runnable from source, so a bug in a
    transformation discovered in March can be fixed and history rebuilt correctly for January —
    something impossible once the transform destroyed the original. The layered architecture that
    grew from this is now standard: a <strong>raw/bronze</strong> layer exactly as landed, a
    <strong>staging/silver</strong> layer that deduplicates and types each source, and
    <strong>marts/gold</strong> layer models shaped for specific business questions. Each layer
    is rebuilt from the one below, and only the bottom layer is allowed to surprise you.
  </p>

  <h2>Orchestration: pipelines are dependency graphs, not scripts</h2>
  <p>
    A production pipeline is dozens of tasks with dependencies ("load payments → rebuild the
    daily revenue model → refresh the dashboard → run the freshness tests → notify failures"),
    scheduled, retried and observed as a whole. <strong>Orchestrators</strong> — Airflow (born at
    Airbnb, 2014), Dagster, Prefect — model this as a directed acyclic graph: each task is a node,
    each dependency an edge, and the scheduler walks the graph, retrying failed nodes with backoff
    and backfilling missed runs after an outage. The mechanism matters because the failure modes
    it manages are temporal: source systems go down at 02:00, a task runs long and collides with
    tomorrow's run, an upstream schema changes on a Friday. A folder of scripts has no answer to
    any of these; an orchestrator answers all three with retries, catchup runs and lineage —
    <em>which tables does this task feed, and which dashboards break if it fails</em>. That last
    question, dependency lineage, is what turns a 3 a.m. incident ("revenue dashboard is wrong")
    from guesswork into a one-command impact analysis.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "data pipelines are just scheduled SQL." The SQL is the easy
    20%. The hard 80% is everything around it: idempotent tasks (safe to re-run twice — because
    they will run twice), late-arriving data, timezone drift between source and warehouse, schema
    evolution, credential rotation, and the organisational question of who is accountable when a
    number is wrong. Teams that budget only for the SQL ship the other 80% as incidents.
  </p>

  <h2>Data quality: tests that run where the data runs</h2>
  <p>
    Software engineering's great insight is that bugs should fail loudly; data engineering
    imports it as <strong>data tests</strong>. The standard battery: <strong>freshness</strong>
    (is today's partition present? has the max timestamp fallen more than an hour behind?),
    <strong>volume</strong> (row counts inside historical bounds — today's 12 million rows versus
    a trailing 30-day average of 11.8 million plus or minus 5%), <strong>schema</strong> (columns
    present, types unchanged, nullability as declared), and <strong>distribution</strong> (no
    sudden null surge, no percentage of negative amounts jumping from 0.1% to 30%). Tools
    (dbt tests, Great Expectations, Soda) express these as declarative assertions that run inside
    the pipeline, and the orchestrator stops downstream propagation on failure — so a broken
    source produces a blocked table and a Slack alert instead of a corrupted dashboard discovered
    by the CFO. The worked case that justifies it all: a payment provider changes a status code
    from "FAILED" to "failed_declined" on a Tuesday. Without tests, dashboards silently report a
    40% drop in failures — celebrated as an improvement. With a distribution test on the status
    column, the change is caught within the hour, and the fix is a mapping table rather than a
    retraction.
  </p>

  <h2>Batch and streaming: choosing the clock</h2>
  <p>
    Every pipeline has a latency budget, and the honest engineering is matching the clock to the
    decision. <strong>Batch</strong> (hourly or daily jobs) covers most analytics: a next-day
    revenue report, a weekly churn model, a monthly compliance extract — batch is simple,
    cheap, replayable and easy to reason about, because each run sees a fixed slice of history.
    <strong>Streaming</strong> (Kafka as the event backbone, Flink or Spark Structured Streaming
    as the processors) processes events within seconds of their arrival, maintaining running
    aggregates over time <em>windows</em> — and inherits a hard new problem: events arrive out of
    order (a phone offline in a tunnel delivers its events late), so the system must decide how
    long to wait (watermarks) and how to revise already-computed results. Fraud scoring, live
    operations dashboards and alerting genuinely need seconds; almost nothing else does, and the
    cost of streaming is real — roughly 5–10× the engineering complexity, plus clusters running
    24/7. The expert default is batch until a measured business decision requires faster, and a
    common hybrid (lambda or kappa architectures) handles the transition deliberately rather
    than accidentally.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "real-time is the mature option." Reverse: streaming is the
    <em>expensive</em> option, justified only when a decision's value decays within minutes.
    Teams that stream everything re-derive batch problems (late data, deduplication, backfills)
    in a harder environment, and usually rebuild a batch layer anyway for month-end correctness.
  </p>

  <h2>Warehouses, lakes and the cost of a query</h2>
  <p>
    The storage layer's design decides everything downstream. A <strong>data warehouse</strong>
    stores modelled, tabular data optimised for analytic reads (columnar formats, partitioning,
    massively parallel scans); a <strong>data lake</strong> stores raw files of any shape
    (Parquet on object storage) for later modelling; the modern "lakehouse" pattern tries to get
    warehouse-like reliability on lake-like cheapness. Two disciplines make the difference
    between a fast platform and a burning one. First, <strong>partitioning and pruning</strong>:
    a query filtered to one day that scans 1/365th of the table costs 1/365th of the money — on
    BigQuery's on-demand pricing of roughly 6 dollars per terabyte scanned, an unpartitioned
    full-table scan habit across 100 analysts running 20 queries a day over a 1-terabyte fact
    table burns on the order of 12,000 dollars a day for answers a partition prune would deliver
    for 33 dollars. Second, <strong>cataloguing and lineage</strong>: a catalog (DataHub,
    Amundsen, Unity Catalog) records what each table means, who owns it and what feeds it, so
    "can I use this column?" has an owner's name attached instead of a shrug.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the data lake replaces the warehouse." A lake holds
  cheap raw bytes with schema-on-read; a warehouse holds modelled, governed tables with
  schema-on-write. Every serious platform ended up wanting both properties, which is why
  the lakehouse pattern (raw landing zones feeding governed table formats like Delta and
  Iceberg) exists — the argument was never either-or, it was which half you cheat on when
  the budget is finite.
  </p>

  <p>
    Schema evolution is the quiet test of a platform's maturity. Tables outlive the
    applications that write them: a column gains a meaning, loses a nullability
    guarantee, or splits in two — and every downstream reader must survive the change.
    The mature patterns are explicit contracts (producers declare the schema they
    write; registry-checked compatibility rules reject breaking producers at deploy
    time, not at 3 a.m. in a dashboard), additive-by-default migrations, and versioned
    tables that let a consumer pin itself to last year's shape. "Move fast and break
    things" fails quietly here: what breaks is not the pipeline but everyone's trust
    in the numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Idempotency and replay: the deep invariant.</strong> The property that makes
    pipelines trustworthy is that any task, re-run on the same input, produces the same result
    (determinism) and the same side effects (idempotency — DELETE-then-INSERT for the partition,
    never append-and-hope). This is what makes backfills safe: fix the code, replay the year,
    history corrected. Streaming systems strain hardest here — exactly-once semantics is achieved
    (Kafka transactions, Flink checkpointing to Chandy–Lamport-style snapshots) by pairing
    deterministic processing with atomic state snapshots, and understanding <em>why</em> it costs
    throughput is the mark of someone who has debugged it live.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Schema evolution and data contracts.</strong> The strongest recent idea is the
    <strong>data contract</strong>: the producing team publishes a versioned schema with
    semantics, freshness SLOs and allowed change policy, and breaking changes require a version
    bump — importing software engineering's API discipline into data. It answers the oldest
    failure in analytics: the upstream team adds a column, renames another "temporarily", and
    downstream silently mis-reports for weeks. The open debate is enforcement (contracts in
    CI for the producer's code versus runtime validation at the boundary) and how much
    governance a self-serve platform can carry before it becomes a bottleneck — the data-mesh
    conversation in one sentence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Incremental processing models (dbt incremental models,
    Flink's streaming-first SQL) blur the batch/stream line; open table formats (Iceberg,
    Delta Lake, Hudi) bring transactions, time travel and schema evolution to plain object
    storage; and the newest layer is <em>semantic</em>: metric layers and semantic models so
    that "revenue" is defined once, in code, and every dashboard agrees. Each generation
    re-solves the same three problems — correctness, freshness, cost — one level higher, which
    is the honest definition of progress in this field.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications; dbt Labs documentation; Kimball, The Data Warehouse Toolkit.</p>
</TopicPage>
